00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,733
These tests are not only for the classroom. A

2
00:00:02,733 --> 00:00:07,389
leap year arrives every four years. A tiler checks

3
00:00:07,389 --> 00:00:10,605
whether eight tiles fit the wall. A bank reads

4
00:00:10,605 --> 00:00:13,051
eleven on long account numbers.

5
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:18,404
Three more divisibility tests. Four, eight and eleven need

6
00:00:18,404 --> 00:00:21,085
more than the last digit, and more than the

7
00:00:21,085 --> 00:00:24,931
digit sum. Zoom in, zoom deeper, and alternate.

8
00:00:25,587 --> 00:00:28,717
The scanner knows six rules: two, three, five, six,

9
00:00:28,717 --> 00:00:32,498
nine and ten. Three new numbers join them: four,

10
00:00:32,498 --> 00:00:36,663
eight and eleven. One digit is not enough for

11
00:00:36,663 --> 00:00:42,358
these. Four and eight look deeper; eleven needs a new idea.

12
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:45,851
Divisibility by four. Seventy six thousand five hundred thirty

13
00:00:45,851 --> 00:00:49,940
two is seventy six thousand five hundred, plus thirty

14
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:52,280
two. The first part is a multiple of one

15
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:55,453
hundred, which divides by four. So it is already

16
00:00:55,453 --> 00:00:58,620
divisible by four. Only thirty two is left, and

17
00:00:58,620 --> 00:00:59,831
it divides by four.

18
00:01:00,727 --> 00:01:03,040
Now use it on fifty six thousand four hundred

19
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:06,170
ninety six. The lens closes on ninety six. Ninety

20
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:09,827
six is four times twenty four, so it passes.

21
00:01:09,827 --> 00:01:13,667
So the whole number divides by four. The test

22
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:15,418
works above one hundred.

23
00:01:16,087 --> 00:01:19,817
Divisibility by eight. One hundred fails, but one thousand

24
00:01:19,817 --> 00:01:23,961
works: eight times one hundred twenty five. Break ninety

25
00:01:23,961 --> 00:01:27,507
three thousand six hundred twenty four into two parts:

26
00:01:27,507 --> 00:01:30,847
ninety three thousand, plus six hundred twenty four. That

27
00:01:30,847 --> 00:01:33,187
part is a multiple of one thousand, so eight

28
00:01:33,187 --> 00:01:36,557
already divides it. And six hundred twenty four divides

29
00:01:36,557 --> 00:01:37,978
by eight exactly.

30
00:01:39,017 --> 00:01:41,677
Do not mix the two. Take two thousand one

31
00:01:41,677 --> 00:01:44,329
hundred four. For four, the last two digits are

32
00:01:44,329 --> 00:01:47,617
zero four: four itself. For eight, the last three:

33
00:01:47,617 --> 00:01:50,917
one hundred four, eight times thirteen. Four looks at

34
00:01:50,917 --> 00:01:53,328
two digits, eight at three.

35
00:01:54,117 --> 00:01:59,397
Divisibility by eleven. Count the places from the right:

36
00:01:59,397 --> 00:02:05,647
odd, even, odd, even. Odd places: one plus two

37
00:02:05,647 --> 00:02:09,893
is three. Even places: two plus one is three.

38
00:02:09,893 --> 00:02:13,928
Compare: three minus three is zero. Zero: eleven divides it.

39
00:02:14,563 --> 00:02:17,788
Now a number that fails. Six thousand five hundred

40
00:02:17,788 --> 00:02:22,711
thirty five. Odd places: five plus five, ten. Even

41
00:02:22,711 --> 00:02:26,560
places: three plus six, nine. Ten minus nine is

42
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:30,394
one. One is not zero and not a multiple of eleven.

43
00:02:31,047 --> 00:02:33,050
Three advanced rules, three clues. For four, zoom into

44
00:02:33,050 --> 00:02:37,227
the last two digits. For eight, zoom into the

45
00:02:37,227 --> 00:02:41,087
last three. For eleven, split the digits into two

46
00:02:41,087 --> 00:02:43,878
alternating groups and compare their sums.

47
00:02:44,757 --> 00:02:47,457
Fifty six thousand four hundred ninety six seats go

48
00:02:47,457 --> 00:02:51,137
into four-seat boxes. Does anything stay empty? The last

49
00:02:51,137 --> 00:02:54,583
two digits, ninety six, divide by four. So the

50
00:02:54,583 --> 00:02:57,268
whole number does. No seat is left over.

51
00:02:57,877 --> 00:03:02,917
Your turn. First, seven hundred twenty six thousand three

52
00:03:02,917 --> 00:03:07,197
hundred fifty two. Pause and decide. Last two digits,

53
00:03:07,197 --> 00:03:11,617
fifty two: four divides it. Last three digits, three

54
00:03:11,617 --> 00:03:15,097
hundred fifty two: eight divides it too. So that

55
00:03:15,097 --> 00:03:18,505
number passes both tests. Now a second number: ten

56
00:03:18,505 --> 00:03:22,483
thousand eight hundred twenty four. Odd places thirteen, even

57
00:03:22,483 --> 00:03:27,228
places two. Thirteen minus two is eleven, so eleven divides it.

58
00:03:27,843 --> 00:03:30,859
One last look. Four: the last two digits. Eight:

59
00:03:30,859 --> 00:03:37,023
the last three digits. Eleven: alternate the places, add

60
00:03:37,023 --> 00:03:42,923
each group, compare. Zoom, zoom more, alternate. Thank you

61
00:03:42,923 --> 00:03:45,514
for watching, and see you in the next lesson.
